-- Expanded OBD-II code content (500-700 words each) for thin-content fix
-- Already applied directly to the live database via Supabase MCP; this file is kept for repo history/reference.

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0011 means the powertrain control module (PCM) has detected that the Bank 1 intake camshaft timing is more advanced than the target the ECU commanded, on an engine with variable valve timing (VVT). Bank 1 refers to the cylinder bank containing cylinder 1. Modern engines use an oil-pressure-actuated camshaft phaser (VVT solenoid) to advance or retard intake valve timing on the fly, improving power, fuel economy, and emissions across the rev range. The PCM continuously compares the camshaft position sensor signal against the crankshaft position sensor signal to calculate actual valve timing, and sets P0011 when the measured advance exceeds the commanded value by more than the calibrated tolerance for a sustained period.

This is almost always an oil-delivery or actuator problem rather than a wiring fault, because the VVT solenoid works by directing engine oil pressure into the phaser to physically rotate the camshaft relative to its drive sprocket. Low oil level, dirty or degraded oil, a clogged VVT oil control valve screen, or a worn phaser itself are the most frequent root causes, which is why this code often shows up on engines that have gone long stretches between oil changes.',
  common_causes = '["Low engine oil level or degraded, sludge-contaminated oil restricting flow to the VVT phaser", "Clogged VVT oil control valve (OCV) screen or solenoid, restricting oil delivery to the phaser", "Faulty camshaft position sensor giving an inaccurate timing reading to the PCM", "Worn or damaged timing chain, chain guides, or tensioner allowing excess slack", "Failing VVT phaser/actuator unit itself, unable to hold the commanded position", "Wiring or connector fault at the VVT solenoid or camshaft position sensor"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light illuminated, sometimes with a rattling or ticking noise from the timing chain area on startup", "Rough idle or a noticeable dip in power under acceleration", "Reduced fuel economy as ignition and valve timing fall out of their optimal window", "Occasional hard starts, especially when the engine is cold and oil hasn''t reached full pressure yet", "In some vehicles, the engine may enter a reduced-power ''limp'' mode to protect the valvetrain"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check the engine oil level and condition first \u2014 top up or perform an oil change if it''s low, dirty, or overdue, since this resolves a large share of VVT codes on its own", "Pull live data on an OBD-II scanner and compare commanded vs. actual camshaft timing at idle and under load to confirm the fault is still active", "Inspect the VVT solenoid wiring harness and connector for corrosion, chafing, or a loose pin", "Remove and inspect the VVT oil control valve screen for sludge or debris blocking oil flow", "If the timing chain has stretched or the phaser itself is worn, a mechanic will need to inspect chain slack and phaser play directly, which usually requires removing the valve cover"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is it safe to keep driving with a P0011 code?", "answer": "Short trips at moderate speed are usually fine, but P0011 means your valve timing isn''t where the engine expects it to be, which can cause uneven wear and, if left unaddressed, contribute to timing chain or phaser damage. It''s worth getting checked within a week or two rather than ignoring it indefinitely."}, {"question": "Will an oil change fix P0011?", "answer": "It''s one of the most common fixes, especially if the oil is old, low, or the wrong viscosity. If the code returns after an oil change and reset, the issue is more likely a clogged VVT solenoid screen or a failing phaser, which needs further diagnosis."}, {"question": "How much does it typically cost to fix P0011?", "answer": "A VVT solenoid replacement is usually a moderate repair cost at most independent shops, while a full phaser replacement costs meaningfully more since it often involves more labor to access. An oil change, if that''s the actual fix, costs far less than either."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0011';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0016 flags a crankshaft position (CKP) to camshaft position (CMP) correlation fault on Bank 1, sensor A. The PCM uses the crankshaft sensor to know exactly where the pistons are in their stroke, and the camshaft sensor to know whether each cylinder is on its intake, compression, power, or exhaust stroke — together they let the ECU fire the correct injector and spark plug at the correct moment. P0016 means these two signals no longer line up within the expected window, which the PCM interprets as either a timing chain that has jumped a tooth, a camshaft actuator holding an incorrect position, or a sensor reporting bad data.

Because this code directly affects ignition and injection timing, it''s rated higher severity than most sensor codes — a serious mismatch can cause the engine to run badly, stall, or in worst cases, cause piston-to-valve contact on interference engines if the timing chain has actually skipped. It''s one of the few OBD-II codes where continuing to drive on worn timing components carries genuine risk of expensive engine damage.',
  common_causes = '["Timing chain or belt that has stretched, or jumped a tooth on the crankshaft or camshaft sprocket", "Worn timing chain guides or a failed tensioner allowing excess chain slack", "Faulty camshaft or crankshaft position sensor giving an inaccurate or noisy signal", "Damaged reluctor ring/tone wheel on the crankshaft or camshaft", "Failing VVT phaser unable to hold commanded camshaft position", "Wiring damage, corrosion, or a poor connector ground affecting either sensor''s signal"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often alongside rough running or a noticeable loss of power", "Engine cranks but starts hard, or occasionally fails to start at all", "Rattling or slapping noise from the timing cover area, especially on startup", "Random or intermittent misfires as ignition timing drifts out of sync", "Stalling at idle or under light load in more advanced cases"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Retrieve freeze-frame data to see engine speed and load conditions when the fault was set, which helps narrow down whether it''s sensor-related or mechanical", "Visually and audibly inspect for timing chain noise with the engine running, which can indicate excessive slack", "Check camshaft and crankshaft sensor wiring and connectors for damage or corrosion before assuming a mechanical fault", "Use a scan tool capable of graphing cam/crank correlation live data to see the actual degree of misalignment", "If mechanical timing is suspected, a technician will need to remove the timing cover to physically verify chain and sprocket alignment against factory timing marks"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Can I keep driving with P0016?", "answer": "This isn''t a code to ignore. If the underlying cause is a stretched or skipped timing chain, continuing to drive risks serious internal engine damage, particularly on interference engines. Get it diagnosed as soon as possible rather than waiting."}, {"question": "Is P0016 always a timing chain problem?", "answer": "Not always \u2014 a faulty cam or crank sensor can trigger the same code without any actual mechanical timing fault. That''s why checking sensor wiring and signal quality first, before assuming the chain has jumped, is the correct diagnostic order."}, {"question": "How much does fixing P0016 typically cost?", "answer": "If it''s a sensor, the repair is relatively affordable. If it''s a genuinely stretched or jumped timing chain, the repair involves substantially more labor to access and correct, since the timing cover, and sometimes the front of the engine, has to come apart."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0016';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0087 indicates that fuel rail pressure is reading lower than the PCM expects, on an engine with a high-pressure direct or common-rail fuel system. The fuel pressure sensor mounted on the rail feeds a live pressure reading back to the ECU, which constantly adjusts the high-pressure fuel pump (and, on some systems, a fuel pressure regulator) to hold the target pressure needed for proper atomization and combustion. When actual rail pressure falls meaningfully below that target for a sustained period, the PCM sets P0087.

Because modern direct-injection and diesel common-rail systems depend on very high fuel pressure — often several hundred to well over a thousand PSI — to atomize fuel properly, a real pressure shortfall doesn''t just hurt performance, it can lead to poor combustion, excess unburned fuel, and potential damage to the high-pressure pump or injectors if run for long periods. This code is treated as high severity for that reason.',
  common_causes = '["Failing high-pressure fuel pump unable to build or hold target rail pressure", "Clogged fuel filter restricting fuel supply to the high-pressure pump", "Weak or failing low-pressure (in-tank) fuel pump starving the high-pressure pump of fuel", "Fuel rail pressure sensor giving an inaccurate low reading despite normal actual pressure", "Leaking or stuck-open fuel injector bleeding pressure off the rail", "Contaminated fuel or debris lodged in the pressure regulator or pump inlet"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often with noticeably reduced power or hesitation under acceleration", "Hard starting or extended cranking before the engine catches", "Engine stalling, especially under load or at higher RPM where fuel demand is highest", "Rough idle or a surging sensation as fuel delivery becomes inconsistent"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check fuel filter condition and replace it if it''s overdue \u2014 a restricted filter is one of the most common and cheapest causes to rule out first", "Connect a scan tool and monitor live fuel rail pressure data against the commanded target across idle and rev-up conditions", "Test low-pressure fuel pump output and fuel volume delivered to the high-pressure pump inlet", "Inspect for external fuel leaks along the rail and injector lines, and check for a stuck-open injector allowing pressure bleed-off"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Can I still drive with a P0087 code?", "answer": "It''s best to avoid it if you can. Running a direct-injection or common-rail engine on insufficient fuel pressure for extended periods risks damaging the high-pressure pump and injectors, which are expensive components to replace."}, {"question": "Is P0087 usually the fuel pump or the sensor?", "answer": "Both are common, which is exactly why a scan tool comparison of commanded versus actual pressure is the right first step \u2014 it tells a technician whether the system is genuinely under-pressurized or whether the sensor itself is misreporting a normal pressure as low."}, {"question": "Why does this code often appear with rough idle?", "answer": "Insufficient fuel rail pressure means injectors can''t deliver fuel as precisely or atomize it as finely as the engine needs, which shows up first as an uneven, surging idle before it becomes a more obvious loss of power under load."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0087';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0102 means the mass airflow (MAF) sensor circuit is reporting a voltage or frequency signal lower than the PCM''s expected minimum, which it interprets as an implausibly low airflow reading. The MAF sensor measures the actual volume and density of air entering the engine, and the PCM uses that reading as the primary input for calculating how much fuel to inject to maintain the correct air-fuel ratio. A signal this low tells the PCM either that almost no air is reaching the engine, or — far more commonly — that the sensor itself, its wiring, or its connector has a fault preventing an accurate reading from reaching the ECU.

Because the MAF sensor sits directly in the intake air stream, it''s also one of the more physically vulnerable sensors on the engine — a thin, hot wire or hot film element that reacts strongly to any oil film, dust, or debris contamination. A large share of P0102 codes trace back to a dirty or oil-fouled sensor rather than an outright electrical failure.',
  common_causes = '["Dirty or oil-contaminated MAF sensor element, often from an oiled aftermarket air filter", "Cracked, loose, or disconnected air intake ducting between the MAF sensor and throttle body, causing unmetered air to enter", "Damaged or corroded MAF sensor wiring or connector", "Faulty MAF sensor itself, worn out or internally failed", "Clogged air filter severely restricting airflow past the sensor", "Poor electrical ground connection affecting the sensor''s reference voltage"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often paired with a noticeable loss of power or hesitation under acceleration", "Rough or unstable idle, sometimes stalling at stops", "Poor fuel economy as the PCM miscalculates the fuel needed for actual airflow", "Hesitation or bogging down when accelerating from a stop", "Black exhaust smoke in more severe cases, indicating an overly rich fuel mixture from a bad low-airflow reading"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Visually inspect the MAF sensor element for visible oil film, dust, or debris \u2014 clean it with dedicated MAF sensor cleaner if contaminated, never with anything else", "Check all intake ducting between the sensor and throttle body for cracks, loose clamps, or disconnected hoses", "Inspect the air filter and replace it if it''s dirty or has been recently oiled", "Check MAF sensor wiring and connector for corrosion or damage, and confirm the sensor is getting proper reference voltage", "Compare live MAF sensor data on a scan tool against expected values at idle and while revving the engine to confirm whether the sensor itself is faulty"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Can I clean a MAF sensor instead of replacing it?", "answer": "Yes, and it''s worth trying first \u2014 MAF sensor cleaner (never anything else, as other solvents can damage the delicate element) resolves a large share of low-reading codes if the cause is oil or dust contamination rather than an actual electrical failure."}, {"question": "Is it safe to drive with P0102?", "answer": "You can generally still drive, but expect reduced performance and poor fuel economy until it''s fixed. If the engine is running very rough or stalling, it''s better to get it looked at sooner rather than later."}, {"question": "Why did P0102 show up after I installed a new air filter?", "answer": "Oiled, reusable performance air filters are a common cause \u2014 the oil used to charge the filter media can migrate downstream and coat the MAF sensor element, which is why many manufacturers specifically warn against using oiled filters with MAF-equipped engines."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0102';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0106 means the manifold absolute pressure (MAP) or barometric pressure sensor signal is outside the range the PCM considers plausible, given other engine data like throttle position and engine speed. The MAP sensor measures vacuum or pressure inside the intake manifold, which the PCM uses — either as the primary load input on speed-density fuel systems, or as a cross-check alongside the MAF sensor on combined systems — to calculate engine load and fine-tune fuel and ignition timing accordingly. A range/performance fault specifically means the signal isn''t outright dead, but it also isn''t tracking correctly with what the engine is actually doing.

This distinguishes P0106 from a simple high or low voltage code: the sensor is producing a signal, but that signal doesn''t correlate properly with throttle position or RPM changes, which usually points to a vacuum leak, a partially clogged sensor port, or a sensor that''s drifted out of calibration rather than a completely dead component.',
  common_causes = '["Vacuum leak in the intake manifold or connected hoses, altering the pressure the sensor actually reads", "MAP sensor vacuum port or hose clogged with carbon or debris, dampening its response", "Faulty or drifting MAP sensor no longer reading accurately across its full range", "Damaged, corroded, or loose MAP sensor wiring and connector", "Throttle body or intake gasket leak allowing unmetered air into the manifold", "Carbon buildup inside the intake manifold restricting normal airflow patterns"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, sometimes with a rough or fluctuating idle", "Hesitation or poor throttle response during acceleration", "Noticeably reduced fuel economy", "Engine may run rich or lean intermittently depending on how the bad signal skews the PCM''s fuel calculation", "Occasional stalling at idle if the reading is bad enough to badly skew fuel delivery"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect all vacuum hoses connected to the intake manifold and MAP sensor for cracks, splits, or loose connections", "Check the MAP sensor''s vacuum port and hose for carbon buildup or blockage that could be damping its response", "Use a scan tool to watch live MAP sensor data while snapping the throttle, confirming the reading rises and falls smoothly and promptly with throttle input", "Inspect the sensor wiring harness and connector for corrosion or a poor connection", "If the sensor and wiring check out, test for intake or throttle body vacuum leaks using a smoke test or carb cleaner spray method around gasket surfaces while the engine idles"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "How is P0106 different from a MAP sensor voltage code like P0107 or P0108?", "answer": "P0107 and P0108 mean the signal is stuck at an implausibly low or high fixed value. P0106 means the signal is moving and changing, but not correlating properly with what the engine''s actually doing \u2014 which usually points more toward a vacuum leak or a drifting sensor than an outright dead one."}, {"question": "Can a vacuum leak really cause this code?", "answer": "Yes, and it''s one of the most common causes. A vacuum leak changes actual manifold pressure independently of what the MAP sensor ''expects'' based on throttle position, so the PCM sees pressure readings that don''t line up with the rest of its data."}, {"question": "Will P0106 hurt my engine if I keep driving?", "answer": "It''s unlikely to cause immediate damage, but a persistently inaccurate MAP reading means your fuel and timing calculations are off, which can hurt fuel economy and drivability, and in a sustained rich or lean condition, contribute to spark plug fouling or catalytic converter stress over time."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0106';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0113 means the intake air temperature (IAT) sensor circuit is reporting a voltage higher than the PCM''s plausible range, which the ECU interprets as an implausibly low or extreme temperature reading (since IAT sensors typically work as thermistors where resistance, and therefore voltage, moves inversely with temperature). The PCM uses IAT data alongside MAF or MAP readings to calculate air density, since colder, denser air needs proportionally more fuel than warm air for the same volume. A high-input fault usually means the circuit has gone to a very high resistance state, which the PCM reads as an unrealistically cold intake temperature regardless of actual conditions.

This is generally one of the less urgent OBD-II codes on this list — it affects fuel trim precision rather than engine safety — but a stuck IAT reading can quietly hurt fuel economy and cold-start behavior for a long time if it isn''t addressed, since the PCM keeps adjusting fuel delivery based on a temperature that isn''t real.',
  common_causes = '["Open circuit or broken wire in the IAT sensor harness", "Corroded, loose, or disconnected IAT sensor electrical connector", "Failed IAT sensor with an internally open or excessively high-resistance element", "Damaged wiring from rodent damage, chafing, or heat exposure near the intake tract", "On sensors integrated into the MAF housing, a failed MAF/IAT combo unit affecting only the temperature portion"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often with no other noticeable drivability symptoms in mild cases", "Slightly rough cold start or extended cranking in colder weather", "Minor reduction in fuel economy as fuel trims compensate for the incorrect reading", "Occasional hesitation on acceleration if the fuel mixture is meaningfully skewed", "In some vehicles, a temporary loss of certain adaptive strategies like knock-based timing adjustments"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Locate the IAT sensor \u2014 either a standalone sensor in the intake tract or combined with the MAF sensor \u2014 and inspect its connector for corrosion or a loose pin", "Use a scan tool to check the live IAT reading against actual ambient/underhood temperature; an implausibly cold reading with the engine warm confirms the fault", "Check continuity and resistance of the IAT sensor itself with a multimeter, comparing against factory specification at known temperatures", "Inspect the wiring harness between the sensor and PCM for damage, especially near heat sources or areas exposed to rodent activity", "Replace the sensor (or MAF/IAT combo unit) if it fails the resistance check, then clear the code and confirm the live reading tracks properly afterward"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is P0113 urgent to fix?", "answer": "Not urgently \u2014 it''s a low-severity code that mainly affects fuel trim accuracy rather than engine safety. That said, it''s worth fixing reasonably soon since it can quietly hurt fuel economy and cold-start performance the longer it''s left."}, {"question": "Can I still pass an emissions test with P0113?", "answer": "No \u2014 any stored Check Engine Light code, including P0113, will typically fail an OBD-II emissions inspection in jurisdictions that check for it, so it needs to be resolved before an inspection."}, {"question": "Is the IAT sensor expensive to replace?", "answer": "Generally one of the more affordable sensor replacements on this list, whether it''s a standalone sensor or combined with the MAF housing, though the combo unit costs more than a standalone sensor alone."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0113';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0121 indicates a range/performance problem with the throttle or accelerator pedal position sensor circuit A — the signal is present and moving, but it isn''t tracking correctly against what the PCM expects based on other inputs like engine RPM, MAP/MAF readings, or the second throttle position sensor most modern throttle bodies use for redundancy. Electronic throttle control systems rely on the throttle position sensor (or sensors, since most use two for cross-checking) to tell the PCM exactly how far the throttle plate has opened, which directly determines commanded airflow and fuel delivery.

Because throttle position accuracy is safety-relevant on drive-by-wire systems, many vehicles will respond to a persistent P0121 by limiting engine power or triggering a limp-home mode, even though the underlying fault often turns out to be something as simple as a dirty or slightly misaligned sensor rather than a serious mechanical problem.',
  common_causes = '["Dirty, worn, or drifting throttle position sensor no longer tracking smoothly across its range", "Carbon or dirt buildup on the throttle plate or bore affecting mechanical throttle movement", "Corroded or damaged throttle position sensor wiring or connector", "Disagreement between the two redundant throttle position sensors due to one sensor failing calibration", "Throttle body that needs relearning/recalibration after a battery disconnect or throttle body cleaning", "Failing electronic throttle body actuator motor causing inconsistent throttle plate movement"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, sometimes with reduced engine power or a limp-home mode limiting RPM", "Hesitation, surging, or an inconsistent feel when pressing the accelerator", "Rough or unstable idle", "Delayed or jerky throttle response", "In more severe cases, the engine may not rev past a limited RPM until the code is cleared"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Use a scan tool to graph both throttle position sensor signals together while slowly opening the throttle by hand (with the engine off and ignition on), checking that both track smoothly and in agreement", "Inspect the throttle body bore and plate for carbon buildup, and clean it with appropriate throttle body cleaner if needed", "Check the throttle position sensor and electronic throttle body connector for corrosion or damage", "Perform a throttle body relearn/adaptation procedure with a scan tool after cleaning or any battery disconnection, since many vehicles require this explicitly", "If sensor signals remain erratic or don''t match after cleaning and relearning, replace the throttle body assembly, since the TPS is usually integrated and not separately serviceable"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Can throttle body cleaning fix P0121?", "answer": "Often, yes \u2014 carbon buildup on the throttle plate can cause inconsistent mechanical movement that confuses the position sensor readings. It''s a common and inexpensive first step before assuming the sensor itself is bad."}, {"question": "Why does my car feel like it has less power with this code?", "answer": "Many vehicles intentionally limit engine power when throttle position data becomes unreliable, as a safety measure to prevent unintended acceleration. This limp mode usually clears once the underlying sensor fault is fixed and the code is cleared."}, {"question": "Do I need to relearn the throttle body after fixing this?", "answer": "On most modern vehicles, yes. After cleaning the throttle body or replacing the sensor, a scan tool relearn procedure tells the PCM the correct baseline (closed-throttle) position, and skipping this step can leave rough idle or hesitation even after the hardware fix."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0121';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0122 means the throttle or accelerator pedal position sensor circuit A is reporting a voltage below the PCM''s expected minimum — a low-input fault, as opposed to the range/performance issue flagged by P0121. This typically points to an actual electrical problem: an open circuit, a short to ground, or a sensor that has failed in a way that pulls its output signal down toward zero volts regardless of actual throttle position. Because the PCM can no longer trust this sensor''s input at all, most vehicles will immediately default to a safe, low-power limp mode rather than attempt to interpret an unreliable signal.

Unlike P0121, which often stems from gradual sensor wear or carbon buildup, P0122 more frequently traces back to a specific wiring fault — a chafed wire, corroded connector pin, or a sensor that has failed outright — making it somewhat more straightforward to isolate with basic electrical testing.',
  common_causes = '["Open circuit or broken wire in the throttle position sensor signal circuit", "Corroded, damaged, or disconnected throttle position sensor connector", "Short to ground somewhere along the sensor wiring harness", "Failed throttle position sensor with an internally shorted or open signal circuit", "Damaged reference voltage supply to the sensor from the PCM", "Water intrusion into the throttle body connector, common after driving through deep water or heavy rain"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, almost always paired with a noticeable loss of power or limp mode", "Engine may be stuck at a fixed, low idle speed regardless of throttle input", "Severely limited acceleration, sometimes to the point the vehicle can barely move under its own power", "Rough or erratic idle immediately after startup", "In some vehicles, the engine may not start at all if the fault is present before startup"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check the throttle body electrical connector first for corrosion, moisture, or a loose pin, since this is the most common and cheapest fix", "Use a multimeter to check for proper reference voltage (typically around 5 volts) reaching the sensor with the key on, engine off", "Check the signal wire for continuity from the sensor connector back to the PCM, looking for an open circuit or short to ground", "Inspect the wiring harness along its full length for chafing, rodent damage, or exposure to water intrusion, particularly near the engine bay firewall", "If wiring and voltage supply check out, replace the throttle position sensor or full electronic throttle body assembly, since the TPS is rarely separately serviceable on modern vehicles"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Why is my car stuck in low power mode with this code?", "answer": "A low-voltage throttle position signal gives the PCM no reliable way to know how much throttle you''re actually requesting, so it defaults to a conservative, low-RPM limp mode as a safety measure until the fault is fixed."}, {"question": "Is P0122 dangerous to drive with?", "answer": "It''s not dangerous in the sense of a safety hazard \u2014 if anything, the limp mode is a protective response \u2014 but the severely reduced power can make the vehicle unsafe to drive in traffic, so it''s best towed or driven minimally to a shop rather than used normally."}, {"question": "Can water cause this code?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 water intrusion into the throttle body connector, often after driving through deep puddles or a car wash, is a surprisingly common cause. Drying out and cleaning the connector sometimes resolves it without needing to replace any parts."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0122';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0128 means the engine coolant temperature hasn''t reached the PCM''s expected regulating temperature within a calibrated time after startup, which almost always points to the thermostat failing to close properly or being stuck partially open. The thermostat''s job is to block coolant flow to the radiator until the engine reaches operating temperature, then open to allow cooling once that threshold is hit. When it''s stuck open — even partially — coolant continuously circulates through the radiator from the moment the engine starts, so the engine takes far longer than normal to warm up, or never fully reaches its target operating temperature at all.

This code is rated lower severity than most on this list because a stuck-open thermostat doesn''t put the engine at risk of overheating — if anything, the opposite. But it does mean the engine runs colder than designed for extended periods, which increases fuel consumption, accelerates internal wear, and can affect cabin heater performance in cold weather.',
  common_causes = '["Thermostat stuck partially or fully open, allowing constant coolant flow to the radiator", "Failed thermostat spring or wax pellet mechanism no longer closing properly as it ages", "Faulty engine coolant temperature (ECT) sensor giving an inaccurate low reading", "Low coolant level affecting the sensor''s ability to read an accurate temperature", "Cooling system air pocket preventing proper coolant circulation and even heating", "Wiring or connector fault at the ECT sensor"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often the only obvious symptom in mild cases", "Engine takes noticeably longer than usual to warm up", "Poor cabin heater performance, especially at idle or low speed in cold weather", "Slightly reduced fuel economy as the engine runs its cold-start enrichment fuel map longer than necessary", "Temperature gauge reading lower than normal or fluctuating rather than settling"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check coolant level and top up if low, then bleed the cooling system if an air pocket is suspected", "Use a scan tool to monitor live coolant temperature from startup, noting how long it takes to reach normal operating temperature (typically within 5-10 minutes of driving)", "Feel the upper radiator hose after the engine has been running for several minutes \u2014 if it stays cool while the engine warms up, the thermostat likely isn''t closing", "Test the ECT sensor''s resistance at a known temperature and compare against factory specification to rule out a sensor fault before replacing the thermostat", "Replace the thermostat if it''s confirmed stuck open, which is the most common fix, then clear the code and confirm normal warm-up time on a subsequent drive"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is P0128 an emergency?", "answer": "No \u2014 it''s one of the lower-urgency codes since it means the engine is running cooler than intended, not overheating. It''s still worth fixing reasonably soon for fuel economy and heater performance, but it won''t strand you or damage the engine if driven for a while longer."}, {"question": "How much does a thermostat replacement typically cost?", "answer": "It''s generally one of the more affordable cooling system repairs, though labor cost varies depending on how accessible the thermostat housing is on a given engine."}, {"question": "Why is my heater blowing cold air with this code?", "answer": "The cabin heater relies on hot engine coolant passing through a heater core to warm the air blown into the cabin. If the thermostat is stuck open and the engine never reaches full operating temperature, the coolant simply isn''t hot enough to produce good heat."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0128';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0135 means the heater circuit for the upstream (Bank 1, Sensor 1) oxygen sensor has malfunctioned. Modern O2 sensors include an internal electric heating element so they reach their operating temperature — typically around 600°F — within seconds of startup rather than waiting for exhaust heat alone, which can take several minutes. Quick heating matters because the PCM needs accurate air-fuel ratio feedback from this sensor almost immediately to properly manage closed-loop fuel control and emissions. P0135 specifically flags a fault in that heater circuit — the sensor''s actual oxygen-sensing function may still work once warm, but the PCM has detected the heater isn''t drawing current or heating as expected.

This is generally a lower-urgency code from a driveability standpoint, since the vehicle will usually still run, but a non-functioning heater circuit delays closed-loop fuel control after every cold start, which increases both fuel consumption and cold-start emissions.',
  common_causes = '["Failed internal heating element within the O2 sensor itself, common as sensors age", "Blown fuse for the O2 sensor heater circuit", "Corroded, damaged, or disconnected O2 sensor electrical connector", "Open or shorted wiring in the heater circuit between the sensor and PCM", "Poor ground connection affecting the heater circuit specifically", "Exhaust leak near the sensor affecting its readings and sometimes triggering related heater timing faults"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often with no immediately obvious drivability change", "Slightly rougher engine running for longer than usual after a cold start", "Minor reduction in fuel economy, particularly on short trips where the engine rarely reaches full closed-loop operation", "Slightly elevated emissions, most noticeable during an emissions test rather than day-to-day driving"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check the fuse associated with the O2 sensor heater circuit and replace if blown", "Inspect the O2 sensor connector for corrosion, damage, or a loose pin", "Use a multimeter to check heater circuit resistance at the sensor connector and compare against factory specification", "Check for continuity along the heater circuit wiring back to the PCM, looking for an open or short", "If wiring and fuses check out, replace the O2 sensor itself, since heater element failure inside the sensor is the most common root cause on higher-mileage vehicles"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Can I drive with a P0135 code?", "answer": "Yes, generally without issue in the short term \u2014 the sensor typically still functions once the exhaust heats it up naturally, just more slowly than intended. It''s worth fixing to restore proper cold-start emissions control and avoid a failed emissions test."}, {"question": "Do all O2 sensors have heaters?", "answer": "All modern (post-1996, OBD-II compliant) oxygen sensors include heater elements. Older vehicles without heated sensors relied entirely on exhaust heat to bring the sensor up to temperature, which is part of why modern systems reach closed-loop operation so much faster."}, {"question": "Will this code cause my car to fail an emissions test?", "answer": "It''s likely to, since any stored Check Engine Light code typically results in an automatic emissions test failure regardless of what the specific code is, separate from whatever tailpipe emissions the car is actually producing."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0135';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0141 is the same fault as P0135, but for the downstream (Bank 1, Sensor 2) oxygen sensor''s heater circuit instead of the upstream sensor. The downstream sensor sits after the catalytic converter and its primary job is monitoring converter efficiency by comparing oxygen content before and after the catalyst, rather than actively managing fuel trim the way the upstream sensor does. It still needs its own heater circuit to reach operating temperature quickly, and P0141 means the PCM has detected that circuit isn''t drawing current or heating properly.

Because the downstream sensor plays a monitoring rather than active fuel-control role, a heater fault here has essentially no effect on how the engine runs day to day — its main real-world consequence is that the PCM can''t properly evaluate catalytic converter efficiency until it''s fixed, and the stored code alone is enough to fail an OBD-II emissions inspection.

One detail worth understanding: because the downstream sensor''s job is purely diagnostic rather than active fuel control, a heater fault here is genuinely lower priority than the same fault on the upstream sensor (P0135). That doesn''t mean it should be ignored indefinitely, but there''s no urgency comparable to codes that affect how the engine actually runs.',
  common_causes = '["Failed internal heating element within the downstream O2 sensor", "Blown fuse for the downstream O2 sensor heater circuit", "Corroded, damaged, or disconnected downstream O2 sensor connector", "Open or shorted wiring in the heater circuit between the sensor and PCM", "Poor ground connection specific to the downstream sensor circuit"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, with no noticeable change in how the engine runs or drives", "No measurable effect on fuel economy, since this sensor doesn''t participate in active fuel trim", "The only practical symptom most drivers notice is failing an OBD-II emissions inspection due to the stored code"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check the fuse associated with the downstream O2 sensor heater circuit and replace if blown", "Inspect the downstream O2 sensor connector \u2014 typically located after the catalytic converter \u2014 for corrosion or damage", "Use a multimeter to check heater circuit resistance at the sensor connector against factory specification", "Check wiring continuity along the heater circuit back to the PCM for an open or short", "Replace the downstream O2 sensor if the heater element itself tests faulty, which is the most common outcome on older vehicles", "If replacing the sensor resolves the code, clear the fault and confirm it doesn''t return after a full drive cycle, since intermittent heater faults can sometimes be traced to a marginal connection that only fails under vibration or heat cycling"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Does P0141 affect how my car runs?", "answer": "No, practically speaking \u2014 the downstream sensor doesn''t participate in active fuel control, so a heater fault here has no noticeable effect on drivability or fuel economy. The main downside is the stored code itself and its effect on emissions testing."}, {"question": "Why does my car need a downstream O2 sensor at all?", "answer": "It exists specifically to monitor catalytic converter efficiency by comparing oxygen levels before and after the catalyst. Without a working heater circuit, the sensor takes longer to reach an accurate reading temperature, delaying that monitoring function."}, {"question": "Is this an expensive fix?", "answer": "Generally one of the more affordable O2-sensor-related repairs, since it''s a single sensor replacement in most cases, assuming the wiring and fuse check out fine first."}, {"question": "Can I tell P0135 and P0141 apart without a scan tool?", "answer": "Not reliably by symptoms alone, since neither code produces a noticeable drivability change on its own \u2014 the code number itself (and which sensor position it references) is the only clear way to know whether it''s the upstream or downstream sensor''s heater circuit at fault."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0141';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0171 means the engine is running leaner than the target air-fuel ratio on Bank 1 — there''s more air relative to fuel than the PCM commanded — and the fuel trim correction needed to compensate has exceeded the calibrated threshold. The PCM constantly fine-tunes fuel delivery using short-term and long-term fuel trim values based on feedback from the upstream O2 sensor (or wideband air-fuel sensor on newer vehicles). When it has to keep adding more fuel than normal, for a sustained period, to bring the mixture back to the target ratio, it sets P0171 to flag that something upstream of its fuel corrections is causing an actual lean condition.

Because a lean condition can stem from anywhere air might be entering the engine without being measured — a vacuum leak, a dirty MAF sensor undercounting airflow, or a fuel delivery problem not supplying enough fuel in the first place — P0171 is one of the more open-ended codes to diagnose, and freeze-frame fuel trim data is usually the best starting point for narrowing down the actual cause.',
  common_causes = '["Vacuum leak in intake hoses, gaskets, or the PCV system allowing unmetered air into the engine", "Dirty or failing MAF sensor undercounting actual airflow, causing the PCM to under-fuel", "Weak fuel pump or clogged fuel filter restricting fuel delivery to match demand", "Clogged or partially clogged fuel injectors reducing delivered fuel volume", "Exhaust leak upstream of the O2 sensor pulling in outside air and skewing the sensor''s reading", "Failing or contaminated upstream O2 sensor giving an inaccurate lean signal"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, sometimes with a hissing sound if a vacuum leak is the cause", "Rough or unstable idle, particularly noticeable at a stop", "Hesitation or lack of power during acceleration", "Reduced fuel economy as the engine compensates with extra fuel trim adjustments", "Occasional stalling at idle in more pronounced cases"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Pull freeze-frame data and check both short-term and long-term fuel trim values to gauge how severe the lean condition is and under what conditions it occurs", "Inspect all vacuum hoses, intake boots, and the PCV system for cracks, disconnections, or leaks, since this is the most common cause", "Clean the MAF sensor with dedicated cleaner and inspect intake ducting for leaks between the sensor and throttle body", "Check fuel pressure at idle and under load against factory specification to rule out a weak fuel pump or clogged filter", "If fuel trims correct significantly with propane or carb cleaner sprayed around vacuum-prone areas (professional smoke testing is safer and more precise), that confirms a vacuum leak as the source"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is P0171 the same as P0174?", "answer": "They''re the same type of fault \u2014 a lean condition \u2014 but on different cylinder banks. P0171 is Bank 1, P0174 is Bank 2. On a V6 or V8 engine, seeing both codes together often points to a shared cause like a vacuum leak or MAF sensor issue affecting the whole engine, rather than two separate bank-specific problems."}, {"question": "Can I keep driving with a lean code?", "answer": "For a short period, usually yes, but a sustained lean condition can cause the engine to run hotter than normal and, over time, contribute to detonation or catalytic converter stress. It''s worth diagnosing within a week or two rather than letting it run indefinitely."}, {"question": "Why does my mechanic want to do a smoke test for this code?", "answer": "A smoke test pumps a visible, harmless smoke into the intake system with the engine off, making even small vacuum leaks immediately visible as smoke escaping from cracks or loose connections \u2014 it''s a far more precise method than guessing based on symptoms alone."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0171';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0174 is the same lean-condition fault as P0171, but affecting Bank 2 rather than Bank 1. On V-configuration engines with two cylinder banks and two separate upstream O2 sensors, the PCM tracks fuel trim independently for each bank, so a lean condition can show up on one bank, both banks, or as separate codes for each depending on the underlying cause. A Bank 2-only P0174 without a matching P0171 often points to something localized to that specific bank — a vacuum leak or injector problem affecting only one side of the engine — whereas both codes appearing together usually points to a shared cause affecting the whole intake or fuel system.

The diagnostic approach and underlying causes for P0174 are essentially identical to P0171, just isolated to the opposite bank, so the same systematic process of checking vacuum integrity, MAF accuracy, and fuel delivery applies here as well.',
  common_causes = '["Vacuum leak specific to the Bank 2 side of the intake manifold or its hoses/gaskets", "Clogged or underperforming fuel injector(s) on Bank 2 reducing delivered fuel volume", "Exhaust leak upstream of the Bank 2 O2 sensor pulling in outside air", "Failing or contaminated Bank 2 upstream O2 sensor giving an inaccurate lean reading", "Shared causes like a dirty MAF sensor or weak fuel pump, which typically also trigger a matching P0171 on Bank 1"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, sometimes with a slight rough idle or hesitation", "Reduced fuel economy as fuel trims compensate for the lean condition", "Mild power loss under acceleration, more noticeable if both banks are affected together", "Occasional rough idle, particularly if the lean condition is significant"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check whether P0171 is also present \u2014 if both banks show lean codes together, focus on shared causes like vacuum leaks, MAF accuracy, or fuel supply rather than bank-specific components", "Inspect Bank 2-specific vacuum lines, intake gaskets, and injector connections for leaks or faults", "Pull freeze-frame and live fuel trim data specifically for Bank 2 to gauge severity and driving conditions when the fault occurs", "Check for an exhaust leak near the Bank 2 upstream O2 sensor, which can pull outside air into the exhaust stream and skew readings", "Test Bank 2 fuel injector performance and clean or replace any that are underperforming relative to their counterparts"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Which bank is Bank 2 on my engine?", "answer": "Bank 2 is the cylinder bank that does not contain cylinder 1, per SAE convention \u2014 but exactly which physical side of the engine that is varies by manufacturer and engine layout, so it''s worth checking a repair manual or diagram specific to your vehicle rather than assuming."}, {"question": "Should I fix P0174 even if the car drives fine?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 a sustained lean condition can quietly increase engine operating temperature and put extra stress on the catalytic converter over time, even if it isn''t obviously affecting how the car drives day to day."}, {"question": "Is this more expensive to fix than P0171 since it''s a different bank?", "answer": "Not inherently \u2014 the cost depends on the actual root cause (a vacuum hose versus an injector, for instance) rather than which bank is affected, though access to Bank 2 components can sometimes involve more labor on certain engine layouts."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0174';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0300 means the PCM has detected random or multiple cylinder misfires that don''t consistently point to any single cylinder — as opposed to codes like P0301-P0304, which flag a misfire isolated to one specific cylinder. The PCM detects misfires primarily by monitoring tiny fluctuations in crankshaft speed between each cylinder''s power stroke; a cylinder that fails to fire, fires weakly, or fires at the wrong time causes a measurable dip in crankshaft acceleration that the PCM logs and counts. When misfires are spread across multiple cylinders rather than concentrated in one, it usually points to a cause affecting the whole engine — fuel delivery, ignition system, or air supply — rather than a single bad spark plug or coil.

This is rated high severity because sustained misfiring allows raw, unburned fuel to pass into the exhaust system and reach the catalytic converter, where it can overheat and permanently damage the catalyst — a far more expensive repair than whatever caused the original misfire. A flashing (rather than steady) Check Engine Light specifically indicates an active, severe misfire and calls for immediate attention.',
  common_causes = '["Worn, fouled, or incorrectly gapped spark plugs across multiple cylinders", "Failing ignition coils, particularly common on coil-on-plug systems as they age", "Vacuum leak causing a lean condition across the whole engine, which can trigger misfires", "Low fuel pressure from a weak fuel pump or clogged fuel filter, starving multiple cylinders", "Clogged or dirty fuel injectors affecting fuel delivery on multiple cylinders", "Low compression from worn piston rings, valves, or a head gasket issue affecting more than one cylinder", "Old or contaminated fuel affecting combustion quality across the engine"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, flashing if the misfire is active and severe enough to risk catalytic converter damage", "Noticeable engine shaking, roughness, or vibration, especially at idle", "Hesitation, jerking, or a loss of power during acceleration", "Poor fuel economy as unburned fuel passes through without contributing power", "A distinct popping or stumbling sensation under load", "Strong fuel smell from the exhaust in more severe cases"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Pull the full code list, not just P0300 \u2014 if individual cylinder misfire codes (P0301-P0304 etc.) are also present, focus diagnosis on those specific cylinders first", "Inspect spark plugs for wear, fouling, or incorrect gap, and check ignition coils for cracks or signs of arcing", "Check fuel pressure against factory specification, and inspect the fuel filter for restriction", "Look for vacuum leaks across the intake system that could cause a widespread lean condition", "If plugs, coils, and fuel pressure check out, perform a compression or cylinder leak-down test to check for a mechanical issue affecting multiple cylinders"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is it safe to drive with a flashing Check Engine Light and P0300?", "answer": "No \u2014 a flashing light means an active misfire severe enough to risk sending unburned fuel into the catalytic converter, which can cause expensive permanent damage within a short time. Stop driving and get it towed or diagnosed as soon as possible rather than continuing to drive."}, {"question": "What''s the difference between P0300 and P0301?", "answer": "P0300 means misfires are occurring across multiple, non-specific cylinders \u2014 pointing toward a system-wide cause like fuel or ignition supply. P0301 (and similarly numbered codes) means the misfire is consistently isolated to one specific cylinder, pointing more toward a localized fault like a single bad spark plug or coil."}, {"question": "Can old gas cause P0300?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 stale or contaminated fuel can burn poorly enough to cause misfires across multiple cylinders, particularly in vehicles that have sat unused for a long period. It''s one of the cheaper possibilities to rule out before assuming a more expensive ignition or mechanical fault."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0300';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0301 means the PCM has detected a misfire specifically and consistently in cylinder 1, distinguishing it from the random multi-cylinder pattern flagged by P0300. Because the fault is isolated to a single cylinder, the diagnostic path is much more direct — the problem almost always lies with a component unique to that cylinder, such as its spark plug, ignition coil, fuel injector, or a mechanical issue like a leaking intake valve or low compression specific to that cylinder, rather than a system-wide fuel or air supply problem.

A reliable diagnostic shortcut for single-cylinder misfires is swapping suspect components — an ignition coil or spark plug — between cylinder 1 and a neighboring cylinder. If the misfire code follows the swapped part to its new cylinder, that component is confirmed faulty. If the misfire stays on cylinder 1 despite the swap, the cause is something else specific to that cylinder''s position, such as its injector or a mechanical issue.',
  common_causes = '["Worn, fouled, or damaged spark plug in cylinder 1", "Failing ignition coil serving cylinder 1", "Clogged, leaking, or electrically failed fuel injector on cylinder 1", "Low compression in cylinder 1 from worn piston rings or a valve sealing issue", "Vacuum leak local to the cylinder 1 intake runner or gasket", "Damaged spark plug wire, on engines still using a distributor-style ignition rather than coil-on-plug"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, flashing if the misfire is severe or sustained", "A noticeable engine shake or rough idle, sometimes more pronounced than a multi-cylinder misfire since one cylinder is contributing no power at all", "Hesitation or a distinct stumble under acceleration", "Reduced power and fuel economy", "In some cases, a faint but distinct popping sound from the exhaust"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect and, if needed, replace the spark plug in cylinder 1, checking for fouling, wear, or incorrect gap", "Swap the ignition coil from cylinder 1 with a neighboring cylinder''s coil and clear the code \u2014 if the misfire follows to the new cylinder, the coil is confirmed faulty", "If the misfire stays on cylinder 1 after the coil swap, check the fuel injector for that cylinder using injector balance testing or resistance checks", "Perform a compression or leak-down test on cylinder 1 specifically to rule out a mechanical cause like worn rings or a valve issue", "Inspect for a localized vacuum leak at the cylinder 1 intake runner or gasket if injector and ignition components all test fine"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Why does my mechanic want to swap parts between cylinders?", "answer": "It''s one of the fastest, most reliable ways to isolate a single-cylinder misfire without expensive equipment \u2014 if the fault follows a swapped coil or plug to its new cylinder position, that component is confirmed as the cause rather than something else specific to that cylinder."}, {"question": "Can a single bad spark plug really cause this whole code?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 a single fouled, worn, or incorrectly gapped spark plug is one of the most common causes of an isolated single-cylinder misfire, and it''s usually the cheapest thing to check and replace first."}, {"question": "Is P0301 more or less serious than P0300?", "answer": "Both carry the same underlying risk to the catalytic converter if the misfire is sustained and severe, but P0301 is generally easier and cheaper to diagnose and fix since the fault is isolated to one specific, known cylinder rather than spread across the engine."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0301';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0302 is the same fault type as P0301 — a misfire consistently detected in a single, specific cylinder — but isolated to cylinder 2 instead. The underlying causes and diagnostic approach are identical: something unique to cylinder 2''s ignition, fuel delivery, or mechanical condition is preventing that cylinder from combusting properly on every cycle, while the rest of the engine runs normally. As with any single-cylinder misfire code, swapping the ignition coil or spark plug between cylinder 2 and a neighboring cylinder is typically the fastest way to confirm whether the fault lies with that component or with something specific to the cylinder''s position (injector, compression, or a local vacuum leak).

One detail worth checking early: on engines where cylinder 2 sits in a position more exposed to heat, vibration, or moisture (depending on the specific engine layout), that cylinder''s ignition components can sometimes wear or fail slightly ahead of others, which is part of why a single-cylinder pattern doesn''t necessarily mean anything unusual is happening — it''s often simply the first component to reach the end of its service life.',
  common_causes = '["Worn, fouled, or damaged spark plug in cylinder 2", "Failing ignition coil serving cylinder 2", "Clogged, leaking, or electrically failed fuel injector on cylinder 2", "Low compression in cylinder 2 from worn piston rings or a valve sealing issue"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, flashing if the misfire is severe or sustained", "Noticeable rough idle or engine shake", "Hesitation or stumbling under acceleration", "Reduced power and fuel economy"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect and replace the spark plug in cylinder 2 if worn, fouled, or improperly gapped", "Swap the ignition coil between cylinder 2 and a neighboring cylinder, clear the code, and see whether the misfire follows the coil to confirm or rule it out", "Check the cylinder 2 fuel injector using resistance or balance testing if ignition components test fine", "Perform a compression or leak-down test specifically on cylinder 2 if fuel and ignition components both check out", "Check cylinder 2''s spark plug gap against factory specification even if the plug looks otherwise healthy, since an incorrect gap alone can cause a consistent misfire without any visible fouling or damage"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Do I need to fix P0302 immediately?", "answer": "If the Check Engine Light is flashing, yes \u2014 that indicates an active misfire severe enough to risk catalytic converter damage. A steady (non-flashing) light still warrants prompt attention, but doesn''t require stopping the car immediately."}, {"question": "Why is it always the same cylinder misfiring?", "answer": "A consistent single-cylinder misfire points to something specific to that cylinder''s hardware \u2014 its spark plug, coil, injector, or internal mechanical condition \u2014 rather than a system-wide issue affecting the whole engine equally, which is what makes single-cylinder codes more straightforward to isolate."}, {"question": "Could this be the same root cause as a P0300 I had before?", "answer": "Possibly \u2014 if a prior P0300 wasn''t fully resolved, or if a component like an aging coil pack has now progressed to consistently affecting one particular cylinder, it can evolve from a random multi-cylinder pattern into a specific single-cylinder code like P0302."}, {"question": "Could a bad ignition coil pack affect multiple cylinders including cylinder 2?", "answer": "On engines that use a shared coil pack rather than individual coil-on-plug units, yes \u2014 a single failing coil pack can cause a misfire pattern that looks isolated to one cylinder even though the root cause is a shared component, which is worth keeping in mind on older or non-coil-on-plug engine designs."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0302';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0303 is a single-cylinder misfire code isolated to cylinder 3, following the same pattern and diagnostic logic as P0301 and P0302. The PCM has determined the misfire consistently traces to this one specific cylinder rather than being spread randomly across the engine, which points the diagnosis toward that cylinder''s spark plug, ignition coil, fuel injector, or a mechanical issue like low compression, rather than a broader fuel or air supply problem affecting multiple cylinders at once. The same coil/plug swap test used for other single-cylinder misfire codes applies here as well, and remains one of the fastest ways to confirm whether an ignition component is at fault.

As with any single-cylinder misfire code, it''s worth resisting the urge to replace all four (or six, or eight) spark plugs and coils at once without confirming which cylinder is actually affected first — while a full ignition tune-up is reasonable maintenance at the right mileage interval, doing it purely in response to one cylinder''s misfire code can mask whether the real cause was something else entirely, like a fuel injector or a mechanical issue.',
  common_causes = '["Worn, fouled, or damaged spark plug in cylinder 3", "Failing ignition coil serving cylinder 3", "Clogged, leaking, or electrically failed fuel injector on cylinder 3", "Low compression in cylinder 3 from worn piston rings or a valve sealing issue"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, flashing if the misfire is severe or sustained", "Noticeable rough idle or engine shake", "Hesitation or stumbling under acceleration", "Reduced power and fuel economy"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect and replace the spark plug in cylinder 3 if worn, fouled, or improperly gapped", "Swap the ignition coil between cylinder 3 and a neighboring cylinder, clear the code, and see whether the misfire follows the coil", "Check the cylinder 3 fuel injector using resistance or balance testing if ignition components test fine", "Perform a compression or leak-down test specifically on cylinder 3 if fuel and ignition components both check out", "If a compression test reveals unusually low compression specifically in cylinder 3, follow up with a wet compression test (adding a small amount of oil to the cylinder before retesting) to help distinguish between worn piston rings and a valve sealing problem"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is a single-cylinder misfire cheaper to fix than a multi-cylinder one?", "answer": "Generally yes, since the diagnosis is more targeted \u2014 you''re checking one cylinder''s components rather than investigating a system-wide cause, which tends to mean less diagnostic labor and often a simpler fix like one spark plug or coil."}, {"question": "Can low compression alone cause P0303?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 if cylinder 3 has worn piston rings, a burnt valve, or a head gasket issue reducing its compression significantly below the other cylinders, it may not combust reliably even with perfectly good spark plugs, coils, and injectors, which is why a compression test is an important step if electrical components check out fine."}, {"question": "Will this code go away on its own?", "answer": "No \u2014 a misfire code reflects an ongoing mechanical or electrical issue and won''t resolve itself. The code may temporarily clear after a certain number of successful drive cycles without a repeat misfire, but the underlying cause needs to be fixed or it will return."}, {"question": "What''s a wet compression test and why does it matter here?", "answer": "Adding a small amount of oil into the cylinder before retesting compression temporarily seals worn piston rings; if compression improves noticeably with oil added, rings are the likely cause, while little to no change points more toward a valve sealing issue instead."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0303';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0304 rounds out the individual cylinder misfire codes on this list, isolated to cylinder 4. As with P0301 through P0303, the PCM has determined this misfire consistently traces to one specific cylinder, pointing the diagnosis toward that cylinder''s spark plug, ignition coil, fuel injector, or a mechanical fault like low compression, rather than a system-wide cause affecting multiple cylinders. The coil/plug swap test remains the standard first diagnostic step, since it quickly distinguishes between a faulty ignition component and something else specific to cylinder 4''s position, such as its injector or internal mechanical condition.

It''s also worth noting that misfire codes are inherently statistical — the PCM counts misfire events over a rolling window of engine revolutions rather than flagging a single missed firing event, so a code like P0304 means a consistent pattern has been detected over time, not just one-off noise. This is part of why the code can sometimes take a drive cycle or two to appear even after a fault has started.',
  common_causes = '["Worn, fouled, or damaged spark plug in cylinder 4", "Failing ignition coil serving cylinder 4", "Clogged, leaking, or electrically failed fuel injector on cylinder 4", "Low compression in cylinder 4 from worn piston rings or a valve sealing issue"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, flashing if the misfire is severe or sustained", "Noticeable rough idle or engine shake", "Hesitation or stumbling under acceleration", "Reduced power and fuel economy"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect and replace the spark plug in cylinder 4 if worn, fouled, or improperly gapped", "Swap the ignition coil between cylinder 4 and a neighboring cylinder, clear the code, and see whether the misfire follows the coil", "Check the cylinder 4 fuel injector using resistance or balance testing if ignition components test fine", "Perform a compression or leak-down test specifically on cylinder 4 if fuel and ignition components both check out", "Check whether the misfire count for cylinder 4 correlates with specific driving conditions (cold start, high load, steady cruise) using scan tool freeze-frame data, since certain fault types are more likely to show up only under specific conditions"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "If I have multiple single-cylinder misfire codes at once, what does that mean?", "answer": "Multiple individual cylinder codes appearing together \u2014 rather than one isolated code or the general P0300 \u2014 often points toward a shared but component-level cause, like an entire set of worn spark plugs reaching end of life together, rather than one isolated failure."}, {"question": "Do I need special tools to diagnose P0304 myself?", "answer": "A basic OBD-II scanner to read live misfire counts per cylinder, a spark plug socket, and a multimeter cover most of the initial diagnostic steps. A compression tester is needed if ignition and fuel components check out fine and a mechanical cause is suspected."}, {"question": "Can a vacuum leak specific to one cylinder cause P0304?", "answer": "Yes, though it''s less common than a spark plug or coil issue \u2014 a leaking intake gasket or cracked vacuum line feeding specifically into cylinder 4''s intake runner can lean out that cylinder enough to cause a consistent, isolated misfire."}, {"question": "Why did this code take a while to show up after I first noticed rough running?", "answer": "The PCM tracks misfires as a count over a rolling window of engine revolutions rather than reacting to a single missed firing event, so it can take a short period of consistent misfiring before the threshold for setting the code is actually reached."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0304';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0325 means the knock sensor circuit on Bank 1 has malfunctioned. The knock sensor is a small piezoelectric device mounted on the engine block that detects the specific vibration frequency caused by detonation (knock) — abnormal, uncontrolled combustion that can occur under high load, with low-octane fuel, or with excessive ignition advance. When the PCM detects knock, it retards ignition timing on the affected cylinder(s) in real time to stop it before it causes damage, then gradually re-advances timing as conditions allow. P0325 means this circuit itself has a fault — an open circuit, a short, or a sensor that''s failed — rather than the sensor actually detecting knock.

Because the knock sensor is a protective safety system, losing it doesn''t just remove a diagnostic input — it removes the PCM''s ability to react to real detonation events happening in the engine, which is why many vehicles respond to this fault by running a more conservative, retarded base ignition timing across the board to protect the engine, at some cost to power and fuel economy.',
  common_causes = '["Failed knock sensor itself, no longer generating a proper signal", "Damaged, corroded, or disconnected knock sensor wiring or connector", "Knock sensor mounting bolt loose or improperly torqued, affecting its ability to detect vibration accurately", "Wiring harness damage from heat exposure, given the sensor''s location near the engine block", "Poor ground connection affecting the sensor circuit"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, sometimes with no other immediately obvious symptom", "Slightly reduced power or fuel economy if the PCM has defaulted to conservative timing as a precaution", "Occasional pinging or knocking sound under load if the sensor fault coincides with an actual detonation event the PCM can no longer correct for", "No symptoms at all in some cases, since the fault may only be caught during a diagnostic scan"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect the knock sensor wiring and connector, located on or near the engine block, for damage or corrosion", "Check that the knock sensor itself is properly torqued to specification, since an improperly tightened sensor won''t transmit vibration accurately", "Test the knock sensor''s signal output with a multimeter or oscilloscope, tapping on the engine block near the sensor to confirm it generates a voltage spike", "Check wiring continuity from the sensor connector back to the PCM for an open circuit or short", "Replace the knock sensor if it fails to generate a proper signal despite good wiring and correct mounting torque"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is it safe to drive with a bad knock sensor?", "answer": "In the short term, yes, since most vehicles compensate by running more conservative ignition timing. But it does mean the engine has lost its main defense against real detonation, so it''s worth fixing reasonably soon rather than relying on that fallback indefinitely."}, {"question": "Will P0325 hurt my fuel economy?", "answer": "It can, modestly \u2014 if the PCM retards base timing as a precaution against undetected knock, the engine won''t be running at its most efficient timing setting, which typically shows up as a small but noticeable dip in fuel economy."}, {"question": "Can using higher octane fuel help with this code?", "answer": "Not directly \u2014 P0325 is an electrical fault in the sensor circuit, not an actual knock event, so fuel octane won''t fix the underlying problem. That said, if you''re also hearing engine pinging, higher octane fuel can reduce actual detonation risk while the sensor issue gets diagnosed and repaired."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0325';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0335 means the crankshaft position (CKP) sensor circuit A has malfunctioned. The crankshaft position sensor is one of the most critical sensors on the entire engine — it''s the PCM''s primary reference for engine speed and exact crankshaft position, used to time ignition spark, fuel injection, and in many cases, to enable the engine to run at all. It works by reading a toothed reluctor ring on the crankshaft (or flywheel) as it spins, generating a signal pattern the PCM decodes into precise rotational position. A fault here — a dead signal, an erratic one, or one that drops out intermittently — can range from a rough-running engine to a complete no-start or a sudden stall while driving.

Because the PCM cannot properly time ignition or injection without reliable crankshaft position data, this is treated as a high-severity code. An intermittent CKP fault is particularly concerning since it can cause the engine to stall unexpectedly at any speed, including on the highway, without warning.',
  common_causes = '["Failed or failing crankshaft position sensor no longer generating a clean signal", "Damaged, corroded, or disconnected CKP sensor wiring or connector", "Excessive air gap between the sensor and the reluctor ring due to a mounting issue", "Damaged or contaminated reluctor ring/tone wheel, often from metal debris or physical damage", "Wiring harness damage from heat, chafing, or age-related insulation breakdown near the sensor", "Weak or failing PCM connection/ground affecting sensor signal interpretation"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, sometimes only after an intermittent stall rather than continuously", "Engine cranks but won''t start, or starts and immediately stalls", "Sudden stalling while driving, with no warning, particularly concerning at highway speed", "Rough idle or hesitation if the signal is intermittent rather than completely absent", "Engine may crank normally but simply never catch, since the PCM can''t time spark or injection without this signal"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check the CKP sensor connector for corrosion, damage, or a loose pin, since this is a common and easily fixed cause", "Use an oscilloscope or scan tool with live CKP data (if available) to check for a clean, consistent signal while cranking or running", "Inspect the sensor''s mounting and air gap against the reluctor ring, correcting if it''s been disturbed during other engine work", "Check for metal debris or physical damage on the reluctor ring/tone wheel itself, which may require removing covers to inspect directly", "Test wiring continuity and resistance along the full circuit from sensor to PCM, checking for intermittent faults by wiggling the harness while monitoring the signal"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Can P0335 cause my car to stall without warning?", "answer": "Yes, and this is one of the more concerning aspects of this code \u2014 an intermittent crankshaft position sensor fault can cause a sudden, unexpected stall at any speed, since the PCM instantly loses its ability to time ignition and injection the moment the signal drops out."}, {"question": "Why won''t my car start with this code?", "answer": "The PCM needs crankshaft position data to know exactly when to fire each spark plug and injector. Without a reliable signal, it often won''t attempt to start the engine at all, or will crank without ever successfully firing."}, {"question": "Is the crankshaft position sensor expensive to replace?", "answer": "The part itself is usually inexpensive to moderate in cost, but labor varies significantly depending on where it''s mounted \u2014 some are easily accessible, while others require significant disassembly to reach, which affects the overall repair cost more than the part price does."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0335';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0401 means the PCM has detected insufficient exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) flow. The EGR system routes a small, controlled amount of exhaust gas back into the intake to lower peak combustion temperatures, which significantly reduces nitrogen oxide (NOx) emissions. The PCM commands the EGR valve open under specific conditions (typically light-to-moderate load, warmed-up engine) and monitors the resulting effect — either through a dedicated EGR position/flow sensor or by watching for the expected change in MAP or O2 sensor readings when the valve opens. When commanded flow doesn''t materialize, P0401 is set.

The most common real-world cause by a wide margin is carbon buildup — EGR passages and valve pintles gradually accumulate carbon deposits from the exhaust gas passing through them, eventually restricting or completely blocking flow even when the valve itself is mechanically able to open. This makes P0401 one of the more predictable, maintenance-related codes on this list rather than a sign of a failed component needing outright replacement.',
  common_causes = '["Carbon buildup clogging the EGR valve, passages, or ports, restricting flow even when the valve opens", "Stuck or mechanically failed EGR valve unable to open properly", "Faulty EGR position or flow sensor giving an inaccurate reading to the PCM", "Clogged EGR cooler (on systems equipped with one), restricting flow", "Vacuum leak or fault in the EGR valve''s control circuit (on vacuum-actuated systems) preventing proper actuation", "Wiring or connector fault on electronically controlled EGR valves"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often with no other obvious drivability symptom at first", "Engine pinging or knocking under load, since reduced EGR flow allows higher combustion temperatures", "Rough idle in some vehicles if the EGR valve is stuck partially open rather than closed", "Increased NOx emissions, typically only noticeable via an actual emissions test rather than daily driving", "Occasional hesitation during specific load conditions where EGR flow is normally active"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect the EGR valve and passages for visible carbon buildup, and clean them thoroughly if clogged \u2014 this resolves a large share of P0401 codes on its own", "Command the EGR valve open with a scan tool while monitoring idle quality; a working, unclogged system should cause a noticeable idle change", "Check the EGR position/flow sensor''s live data against expected values when the valve is commanded open", "Inspect wiring and connectors on electronically controlled EGR valves for damage or corrosion", "On vacuum-actuated systems, check the vacuum line and control solenoid for leaks or a stuck-closed condition preventing valve actuation"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is P0401 usually a cheap fix?", "answer": "Often, yes \u2014 carbon buildup is by far the most common cause, and cleaning the EGR valve and passages is a relatively affordable repair. Full EGR valve replacement costs more but is still generally moderate compared to many other engine repairs."}, {"question": "Will this code cause knocking or pinging?", "answer": "It can \u2014 the EGR system exists specifically to lower peak combustion temperatures, so when flow is restricted, the engine can run hotter under load and become more prone to detonation, which sometimes shows up as an audible knock or ping, especially on acceleration."}, {"question": "Can I just disable the EGR system instead of fixing it?", "answer": "This isn''t recommended and is illegal in most jurisdictions for emissions-controlled vehicles on public roads, since it removes a required emissions control device. It also risks increased engine knock and long-term wear from higher combustion temperatures, so proper repair is the better path."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0401';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0420 means the catalytic converter on Bank 1 is operating below the efficiency threshold the PCM expects. The PCM determines this by comparing the signal patterns from the upstream O2 sensor (before the catalyst) against the downstream O2 sensor (after the catalyst). A healthy catalytic converter smooths out and delays the oxygen content fluctuations passing through it, so the downstream sensor''s signal should look noticeably calmer and more stable than the upstream sensor''s rapidly switching signal. When the two signals start looking too similar — meaning the catalyst isn''t effectively processing exhaust gases — the PCM sets P0420.

While a genuinely worn-out catalytic converter is a real and fairly common cause, especially on higher-mileage vehicles, P0420 is also one of the most frequently misdiagnosed codes, because almost anything that sends the catalyst an already-bad air-fuel mixture — a misfire, a failing O2 sensor, or an exhaust leak — can trigger the same efficiency-below-threshold reading without the catalytic converter itself being at fault at all.',
  common_causes = '["Genuinely worn out or damaged catalytic converter no longer processing exhaust gases efficiently", "Failing upstream or downstream O2 sensor giving inaccurate readings that mimic a converter efficiency problem", "Engine misfire sending unburned fuel into the catalyst, which can damage it over time and skew efficiency readings even before outright failure", "Exhaust leak upstream of the catalytic converter, introducing extra oxygen that confuses the efficiency calculation", "Persistent rich or lean fuel condition (from causes like P0171/P0174/P0300) gradually damaging the catalyst", "Engine oil or coolant consumption contaminating and damaging the catalytic converter over time"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, typically with no other obvious symptom if the catalyst itself is the actual cause", "Reduced fuel economy and/or performance if the root cause is an underlying misfire or fuel trim issue rather than the catalyst alone", "A sulfur or rotten-egg smell from the exhaust in some cases of catalyst failure", "Rattling noise from underneath the vehicle if the catalytic converter''s internal substrate has physically broken apart", "Failing an emissions test, which is often how this problem is first discovered"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check for any other stored or pending codes first \u2014 misfire codes, fuel trim codes, or O2 sensor codes \u2014 since fixing an underlying cause often resolves P0420 without touching the catalytic converter at all", "Inspect the exhaust system upstream of the catalytic converter for leaks that could be introducing extra oxygen into the readings", "Compare live upstream and downstream O2 sensor waveforms on a scan tool capable of graphing them together; a healthy catalyst shows a clear difference in signal activity between the two", "Physically inspect and, if accessible, gently tap the catalytic converter housing to check for loose or rattling internal substrate, a sign of physical damage", "Replace the catalytic converter only after ruling out sensor faults, misfires, and exhaust leaks as the actual root cause, since it''s one of the more expensive parts on this list"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Should I replace the catalytic converter as soon as I see P0420?", "answer": "Not immediately \u2014 this code is frequently caused by something else entirely (a misfire, a failing O2 sensor, an exhaust leak) sending the catalyst bad signals or bad exhaust gas. Ruling those out first can save you from replacing an expensive part that wasn''t actually the problem."}, {"question": "Can a P0300 misfire code cause P0420 to also appear?", "answer": "Yes, and it''s a very common combination \u2014 unburned fuel from a misfire reaching the catalytic converter both skews its efficiency readings and can physically damage the catalyst over time if the misfire isn''t fixed promptly."}, {"question": "How much does catalytic converter replacement typically cost?", "answer": "It''s one of the more expensive single-part repairs on this list, particularly on vehicles where the converter is integrated into the exhaust manifold rather than a separate bolt-on unit, and prices vary significantly by vehicle and by local catalytic converter material costs."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0420';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0430 is the same catalytic converter efficiency fault as P0420, but for Bank 2 instead of Bank 1. On V-configuration engines with two separate exhaust banks and their own catalytic converters, each side is monitored independently, so it''s entirely possible for only one bank''s catalyst to show reduced efficiency while the other tests fine — often because that specific bank has an issue (a misfire, an O2 sensor fault, or an exhaust leak) that the other bank doesn''t share. As with P0420, the underlying cause is frequently something feeding the Bank 2 catalyst bad exhaust gas or bad sensor data, rather than the catalytic converter itself being worn out.',
  common_causes = '["Genuinely worn out or damaged Bank 2 catalytic converter", "Failing Bank 2 upstream or downstream O2 sensor giving inaccurate readings", "Engine misfire on a Bank 2 cylinder sending unburned fuel into that side''s catalyst", "Exhaust leak upstream of the Bank 2 catalytic converter", "Persistent rich or lean condition specific to Bank 2 gradually damaging that side''s catalyst"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, typically with no other obvious symptom if the catalyst is the sole cause", "Reduced performance or fuel economy if an underlying Bank 2 misfire or fuel trim issue is the actual root cause", "Failing an emissions test", "Rattling noise from underneath the vehicle on the Bank 2 side if the catalyst substrate has physically broken apart"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check for other stored codes specific to Bank 2 \u2014 misfire codes, P0174 fuel trim codes, or Bank 2 O2 sensor codes \u2014 since resolving those often clears P0430 without needing to touch the catalytic converter", "Inspect the Bank 2 exhaust system for leaks upstream of the catalytic converter", "Compare live Bank 2 upstream and downstream O2 sensor waveforms on a scan tool to assess actual catalyst performance", "Physically inspect the Bank 2 catalytic converter for signs of physical damage or a rattling internal substrate", "Replace the Bank 2 catalytic converter only after ruling out sensor, misfire, and exhaust leak causes specific to that bank"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Why do I have P0430 but not P0420?", "answer": "It means Bank 2''s catalyst specifically is showing reduced efficiency while Bank 1 is testing fine, which usually points to a cause isolated to that bank \u2014 a Bank 2 misfire, a Bank 2 O2 sensor fault, or a Bank 2-side exhaust leak \u2014 rather than something affecting the whole engine equally."}, {"question": "Do I need to replace both catalytic converters if only one bank shows a code?", "answer": "No \u2014 if only Bank 2 is flagging an efficiency problem and Bank 1 is testing normally, there''s no need to replace the Bank 1 converter as well. Replace only the side that''s actually confirmed faulty after ruling out other causes."}, {"question": "Is diagnosing P0430 different from P0420?", "answer": "The diagnostic logic is identical, just applied to the opposite bank''s sensors and exhaust components \u2014 the same principle of ruling out misfires, sensor faults, and exhaust leaks before assuming the catalytic converter itself needs replacing applies equally to both codes."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0430';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0442 means the PCM''s evaporative emission control (EVAP) system leak test has detected a small leak. The EVAP system captures fuel vapor from the gas tank rather than venting it to the atmosphere, storing it in a charcoal canister until the engine can safely draw it in and burn it during normal operation. To confirm the whole sealed system — tank, cap, hoses, canister, and valves — remains properly sealed, the PCM periodically runs a self-test, either by monitoring how a small vacuum or pressure applied to the system holds over time, and flags P0442 when it detects a small leak, distinct from the larger leak flagged by P0455.

The single most common cause by a wide margin is a loose, damaged, or missing gas cap, which is why many technicians check the cap first before investigating anything else — it costs nothing to check and resolves a large share of these codes immediately.',
  common_causes = '["Loose, cracked, or missing fuel filler cap failing to seal properly", "Cracked or deteriorated EVAP hoses, particularly rubber sections that harden and crack with age", "Faulty EVAP purge valve or vent valve not sealing correctly when closed", "Small crack or pinhole in the charcoal canister housing", "Damaged or corroded fuel tank pressure sensor giving an inaccurate leak-test reading", "Small crack in the fuel filler neck or tank itself, less common but possible on older vehicles"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, with no noticeable effect on how the vehicle drives", "Occasionally a faint fuel smell if the leak is more than truly minor", "No performance, power, or fuel economy impact in the vast majority of cases", "The only practical consequence for most drivers is failing an OBD-II emissions inspection"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check that the gas cap is properly and fully tightened, and inspect it for cracks or a damaged sealing gasket, replacing it if needed \u2014 this alone resolves a large share of P0442 codes", "Visually inspect all accessible EVAP hoses for cracking, especially rubber sections near the engine bay and under the vehicle", "Use a scan tool to command the EVAP system''s self-test and review live tank pressure data during the test cycle", "If available, use a smoke machine connected to the EVAP system to visually find the leak location without needing to physically inspect every hose and connection", "Check the purge and vent valves for proper sealing and operation if no leak is found through visual inspection or smoke testing"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Should I just check my gas cap first?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 it''s the single most common cause of P0442, costs nothing to check, and takes less than a minute. Make sure it''s tightened until it clicks (on caps with a click mechanism) and inspect the rubber seal for cracks or damage."}, {"question": "Is P0442 urgent to fix?", "answer": "Not urgently \u2014 it has essentially no effect on how the vehicle runs or performs. It''s still worth fixing since it will cause an emissions test failure and represents a small amount of fuel vapor escaping rather than being captured and burned as designed."}, {"question": "Why is EVAP leak testing so hard to diagnose without special tools?", "answer": "A ''small'' leak in EVAP terms can be an opening as tiny as 0.040 inches in diameter, which is far too small to reliably find by eye alone across dozens of feet of hose and multiple components \u2014 this is exactly why smoke testing, which makes even pinhole leaks visible, is the preferred professional diagnostic method."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0442';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0455 is the same EVAP system leak fault as P0442, but flags a large leak rather than a small one. Because the PCM''s leak test can distinguish leak severity based on how quickly pressure or vacuum bleeds off during the test cycle, a large leak — generally interpreted as an opening roughly the size of a quarter-inch (about 6mm) diameter or larger — points toward more obvious culprits than the pinhole-sized leaks associated with P0442, most commonly a gas cap that''s missing entirely, a disconnected hose, or a more significant crack somewhere in the system.

Because the fault is larger and generally easier to spot, P0455 is often quicker and cheaper to diagnose than P0442 — a visual inspection alone frequently finds the source without needing smoke testing or other specialized diagnostic equipment.

One useful diagnostic distinction: because P0455 indicates a leak large enough that the system essentially can''t hold any meaningful vacuum or pressure at all during the self-test, technicians can often confirm the general leak category almost immediately from the test results alone, before even opening the hood — which is part of why this code, despite sounding similar to P0442, often resolves faster in the shop.',
  common_causes = '["Missing, badly damaged, or completely failed-to-seal fuel filler cap", "Disconnected or badly cracked/split EVAP hose", "Failed EVAP purge or vent valve stuck open", "Significant crack in the charcoal canister housing", "Damaged fuel tank pressure sensor connector or wiring"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, with no noticeable effect on drivability", "Possible mild fuel smell, more noticeable than with a small (P0442) leak given the larger opening", "No performance or fuel economy impact in most cases", "Failing an OBD-II emissions inspection"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check first for a missing or badly damaged gas cap \u2014 the most common and simplest cause to rule out", "Visually inspect EVAP hoses for obvious disconnection, splitting, or damage, since a large leak is often easy to spot without special equipment", "Command the EVAP system self-test with a scan tool and monitor how quickly tank pressure changes, confirming the leak size", "Check the purge and vent valves for a stuck-open condition preventing the system from sealing during the test", "Use a smoke test if visual inspection doesn''t turn up an obvious cause, though large leaks are typically found without needing one", "Check the fuel filler neck and surrounding seal for physical damage if the gas cap itself tests fine, since a damaged filler neck can produce a large-leak result identical to a missing or faulty cap"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "How is P0455 different from P0442?", "answer": "Both are EVAP system leak codes, but P0455 indicates a larger leak (generally interpreted as roughly quarter-inch diameter or bigger) versus the much smaller pinhole-level leak flagged by P0442. Larger leaks are usually easier and faster to physically locate."}, {"question": "Can I drive normally with P0455?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 like P0442, this code has no effect on engine performance or safety. It simply means fuel vapor is escaping the sealed EVAP system rather than being captured, which mainly matters for emissions compliance rather than drivability."}, {"question": "Is a large EVAP leak more expensive to fix than a small one?", "answer": "Not necessarily \u2014 since large leaks are often easier to visually locate (a missing cap or a clearly disconnected hose, for example), they can actually be quicker and cheaper to diagnose than a small pinhole leak requiring smoke testing to find."}, {"question": "Could a recent gas station fill-up cause this code?", "answer": "Yes, indirectly \u2014 if the cap wasn''t replaced properly and fully tightened after refueling, that alone is enough to trigger a large EVAP leak code on the very next drive cycle, which is why checking the cap is always the first and easiest step."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0455';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0505 means the PCM has detected a malfunction in the idle air control (IAC) system, which is responsible for maintaining a steady, correct idle speed without the driver''s foot on the accelerator. On engines with a dedicated IAC valve, this component controls a small bypass of air around the closed throttle plate, adjusting airflow to compensate for engine load changes at idle — things like the AC compressor kicking on, power steering demand while turning at a stop, or the engine warming up. On modern electronic throttle body systems, idle is instead controlled by precisely positioning the throttle plate itself, and P0505 can reflect a broader fault in that idle control strategy rather than a physical IAC valve specifically.

Because idle control affects the engine constantly whenever the vehicle isn''t moving, this code is highly noticeable in daily driving even though it''s rated only medium severity from a mechanical risk standpoint — an unstable idle is inconvenient and can be embarrassing at a stoplight, but it''s not immediately damaging to the engine.',
  common_causes = '["Carbon buildup inside the IAC valve or its passage, restricting or sticking the valve''s movement", "Failed or sticking IAC valve unable to respond properly to PCM commands", "Vacuum leak affecting idle airflow independent of what the IAC valve is commanding", "Dirty throttle body, particularly around the throttle plate, causing airflow inconsistency at idle", "Corroded or damaged IAC valve electrical connector", "On electronic throttle systems, a throttle body needing relearn/recalibration"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, almost always with a noticeable rough, unstable, or fluctuating idle", "Idle speed too high or too low compared to normal", "Engine stalling at stops, particularly when coming to a stop with the AC on or while turning the steering wheel", "Idle speed that hunts or surges up and down rather than settling", "Occasional stalling right after startup before the engine has fully warmed up"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Inspect and clean the IAC valve and its passage of carbon buildup, which resolves a large share of idle control codes", "Clean the throttle body, particularly around the throttle plate edges where carbon commonly accumulates", "Check the IAC valve''s electrical connector for corrosion or damage", "Inspect for vacuum leaks near the intake manifold and throttle body that could be adding uncontrolled airflow at idle", "On electronic throttle body vehicles, perform a throttle body relearn/idle relearn procedure with a scan tool after cleaning, since this step is often required for idle to stabilize properly afterward"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Can cleaning the throttle body fix P0505?", "answer": "Often, yes \u2014 carbon buildup on the IAC valve or throttle plate is one of the most common causes, and a proper cleaning followed by an idle relearn procedure resolves a large portion of these codes without needing to replace any parts."}, {"question": "Why does my car stall when I stop with the AC running?", "answer": "The AC compressor adds a real mechanical load to the engine, and the idle control system needs to compensate for that extra load to keep idle speed stable. If the IAC valve or throttle control system can''t respond properly, that added load can be enough to stall the engine at idle."}, {"question": "Do I need to do anything after cleaning the throttle body?", "answer": "On most modern vehicles, yes \u2014 a throttle body or idle relearn procedure through a scan tool tells the PCM the correct baseline idle position after cleaning, and skipping this step often leaves the idle just as rough as before, even though the actual carbon buildup has been removed."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0505';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0700 is a general request from the transmission control module (TCM) asking the engine''s PCM to illuminate the Check Engine Light because the transmission system itself has detected a fault. It''s essentially a pointer code rather than a description of a specific problem — it tells you the transmission control system has an issue, but the actual detail lives in a separate, transmission-specific diagnostic trouble code that will also be stored alongside P0700. On many vehicles, especially those with a shared PCM/TCM architecture, this code appears automatically whenever any other transmission-related fault is present.

This is treated as high severity mainly because of what it typically signals rather than what it directly describes — many of the specific transmission codes that trigger P0700 relate to shift quality, torque converter operation, or internal transmission sensors, and ignoring them can allow a developing transmission problem to progress into a much more expensive repair.',
  common_causes = '["An underlying, more specific transmission fault code (such as a shift solenoid, speed sensor, or torque converter code) is also present", "Low or degraded transmission fluid affecting shift quality and internal sensor readings", "Failing transmission speed sensor(s) sending inconsistent data to the TCM", "Wiring or connector issues within the transmission wiring harness", "Internal transmission mechanical wear affecting shift solenoids or valve body operation", "Software or communication fault between the TCM and PCM in some vehicle architectures"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often paired with a separate transmission-specific code", "Harsh, delayed, or erratic gear shifts", "Transmission may enter ''limp mode,'' locking into a single gear (often 2nd or 3rd) to protect itself from further damage", "Slipping sensation between gears, or the transmission failing to shift at the expected point", "In some cases, no noticeable symptom beyond the warning light itself, particularly if caught early"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Pull the full code list rather than just P0700 \u2014 the accompanying, more specific transmission code is what actually points to the root cause", "Check transmission fluid level and condition, since low or degraded fluid is one of the most common and cheapest causes to rule out", "Inspect transmission wiring harness and connectors for damage, especially at points where the harness flexes or is exposed to heat", "Use a scan tool capable of communicating with the TCM specifically to pull live transmission sensor data and any transmission-specific fault codes", "If a specific internal component (like a shift solenoid) is identified, a transmission specialist should evaluate whether it''s a standalone repair or a sign of broader internal wear"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "What does P0700 actually tell me on its own?", "answer": "Not much by itself \u2014 it''s essentially a flag saying ''check the transmission system for a more specific fault.'' The real diagnostic information is in whatever additional transmission-specific code is stored alongside it, so that''s what a technician needs to actually pinpoint the problem."}, {"question": "Is P0700 always a serious transmission problem?", "answer": "Not necessarily \u2014 it can range from something as simple as low transmission fluid to a genuinely failing internal component. The severity depends entirely on the specific accompanying code, which is why pulling the full code list matters so much here."}, {"question": "Can I keep driving with P0700 and a limp mode transmission?", "answer": "You can drive cautiously for a short distance, but a transmission stuck in limp mode is running in a single, non-optimal gear as a protective measure, and continuing to drive extensively in that state isn''t recommended. Getting it diagnosed promptly is the safer choice."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0700';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0716 means the input (also called turbine) speed sensor circuit inside the automatic transmission is reporting a range or performance problem — the signal is present but not behaving as the TCM expects relative to engine speed and other transmission data. The input speed sensor measures how fast the transmission''s input shaft (connected to the torque converter''s turbine) is spinning, which the TCM compares against the engine''s output and the transmission''s output shaft speed to calculate things like torque converter slip and to time shifts precisely. A range/performance fault here typically points to either a sensor generating a noisy or erratic signal, or an internal mechanical issue affecting the shaft itself.

It''s worth noting this code is sometimes confused with a similar but distinct fault, P0715, which covers the same input speed sensor but flags a different type of electrical problem (signal intermittent or erratic rather than a plausibility mismatch). Both point to the same physical sensor and wiring, so the diagnostic approach overlaps significantly regardless of which specific code is present.',
  common_causes = '["Failing or degraded input/turbine speed sensor no longer producing a clean signal", "Damaged, corroded, or loose input speed sensor wiring or connector", "Low or contaminated transmission fluid affecting internal component behavior and sensor readings", "Damaged reluctor ring or tone wheel that the sensor reads to determine speed", "Internal transmission wear affecting the input shaft or connected components"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often paired with harsh, delayed, or inconsistent shifting", "Transmission may enter limp mode, restricting available gears", "Slipping sensation, particularly during shifts", "Erratic shift points that don''t match normal driving conditions", "In some cases, a delay in the transmission engaging after shifting into drive"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check transmission fluid level and condition first, since degraded or low fluid affects overall transmission behavior including sensor accuracy", "Inspect the input speed sensor connector for corrosion or damage", "Use a scan tool to compare live input speed sensor data against engine RPM and output speed sensor data during a test drive", "Check wiring continuity from the sensor to the TCM for damage or an intermittent connection", "If the sensor and wiring test fine, a transmission specialist should evaluate whether internal wear on the input shaft or connected components is affecting sensor readings", "If the vehicle also has a separate output speed sensor code, prioritize resolving that one first, since a genuinely faulty output sensor can sometimes cause the TCM to misinterpret otherwise normal input sensor data as a range/performance fault"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is P0716 a sensor problem or an internal transmission problem?", "answer": "It can be either, which is why proper diagnosis matters before assuming the worst. A faulty sensor or damaged wiring is a relatively straightforward and affordable fix, while genuine internal transmission wear is a much bigger repair \u2014 a scan tool comparison of sensor data against expected values usually points toward which is more likely."}, {"question": "Can low transmission fluid cause this code?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 low or degraded fluid can affect how transmission components move and behave internally, which can throw off sensor readings even when the sensor itself is functioning correctly. Checking fluid level and condition is always a sensible early step."}, {"question": "Should I stop driving if my transmission is in limp mode?", "answer": "Limp mode is a protective response, so driving cautiously to a repair shop is generally fine, but extended driving in limp mode isn''t recommended since it may mean an underlying issue is actively getting worse."}, {"question": "Is this the same as P0715?", "answer": "They reference the same input/turbine speed sensor, but P0715 typically flags an intermittent or erratic signal while P0716 flags a range/performance mismatch against other transmission data \u2014 in practice, both usually point toward checking the same sensor, wiring, and transmission fluid condition."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0716';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'P0740 means the torque converter clutch (TCC) circuit has malfunctioned. Inside an automatic transmission''s torque converter, a mechanical lock-up clutch engages at cruising speed to create a direct, slip-free connection between the engine and transmission, improving fuel economy compared to running through fluid coupling alone. The TCC solenoid controls when this clutch engages and disengages based on TCM commands, and P0740 flags a general electrical or performance fault in that circuit — the clutch isn''t engaging, disengaging, or holding properly as commanded.

Because the torque converter clutch engages and disengages constantly during normal driving, a faulty circuit here is immediately noticeable and, if the clutch is slipping rather than locking properly, generates excess heat inside the transmission that can accelerate internal wear if left unaddressed for an extended period.',
  common_causes = '["Failed or sticking torque converter clutch solenoid", "Low or degraded transmission fluid, which is critical to proper torque converter clutch operation", "Damaged, corroded, or disconnected TCC solenoid wiring or connector", "Worn torque converter clutch material no longer able to hold lock-up properly", "Internal transmission valve body issue affecting hydraulic pressure to the TCC circuit", "Faulty transmission fluid temperature sensor causing improper TCC engagement timing"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often with a noticeable shudder or vibration at cruising speed when the torque converter should be locking up", "Reduced fuel economy, since the torque converter isn''t achieving its efficient locked state properly", "Transmission may enter limp mode in more severe cases", "Engine RPM flaring or surging unexpectedly at steady highway speed", "Overheating transmission fluid if the clutch is slipping rather than locking, generating excess friction heat"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check transmission fluid level and condition, since proper fluid is essential for correct torque converter clutch engagement", "Use a scan tool to monitor TCC commanded state versus actual engagement, and check for RPM flare during the moment lock-up should occur", "Inspect the TCC solenoid wiring and connector for damage or corrosion", "Test the TCC solenoid''s resistance against factory specification to check for an internal electrical fault", "If fluid, wiring, and solenoid all test fine, a transmission specialist should evaluate for internal wear on the torque converter clutch itself or a valve body hydraulic issue"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Why does my car shudder at highway speed with this code?", "answer": "That shudder is a classic sign of the torque converter clutch engaging inconsistently or slipping rather than locking cleanly \u2014 instead of a smooth, direct mechanical connection, the clutch is partially grabbing and releasing, which transmits as a vibration you can feel through the vehicle."}, {"question": "Can this code damage my transmission if I keep driving?", "answer": "A slipping torque converter clutch generates extra heat inside the transmission, and sustained overheating is one of the more common contributors to accelerated internal transmission wear, so it''s worth addressing this reasonably promptly rather than driving on it indefinitely."}, {"question": "Is a new transmission fluid and filter service worth trying first?", "answer": "Yes, especially if the fluid hasn''t been serviced in a long time or looks degraded \u2014 proper fluid condition is directly tied to how well the torque converter clutch can engage, and a fluid service is far cheaper than diagnosing further before ruling this out."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'P0740';

UPDATE obd_codes SET
  description = 'U0100 means the vehicle has lost communication with the engine control module (ECM) or powertrain control module (PCM) over the vehicle''s internal communication network — typically the CAN (Controller Area Network) bus that lets all the vehicle''s modules talk to each other. This is a network-level code rather than a component-specific fault: another module (often the body control module or instrument cluster) is reporting that it can no longer receive expected messages from the engine control module, which could mean the ECM/PCM has failed, lost power, or — more commonly — that the wiring or connectors carrying that communication have a fault somewhere along the bus.

Because so many of a modern vehicle''s systems depend on the ECM/PCM sharing data across the network — everything from the instrument cluster''s tachometer to traction control to sometimes even basic dashboard warning lights — a genuine loss of communication is treated as critical severity and can affect far more than just engine performance.',
  common_causes = '["Blown fuse or loss of power/ground to the ECM/PCM itself", "Damaged, corroded, or disconnected wiring on the CAN bus communication lines", "Failed ECM/PCM unable to communicate on the network even if otherwise powered", "Corrosion or damage at a CAN bus connector or splice point affecting the whole network segment", "A different failed module on the same CAN bus network disrupting communication for all connected modules", "Water intrusion into a connector, particularly common after driving through deep water or flooding"]'::jsonb,
  symptoms = '["Check Engine Light on, often alongside multiple other unrelated warning lights simultaneously", "Instrument cluster may show inaccurate or missing data \u2014 such as no tachometer reading \u2014 since it relies on ECM/PCM broadcast data", "Engine may not start, or may run in a severely limited state", "Multiple systems behaving oddly at once (transmission, traction control, cruise control), since they all depend on shared network data", "Vehicle may fail to communicate with a diagnostic scan tool at all when trying to read other modules"]'::jsonb,
  diagnostic_steps = '["Check the ECM/PCM''s power and ground connections, along with relevant fuses, first \u2014 this rules out the simplest and most common cause", "Inspect the main engine wiring harness and connectors for visible damage, corrosion, or water intrusion", "Use a multimeter or oscilloscope to check for proper CAN bus voltage and signal integrity on the communication lines", "Check whether other modules on the network are also reporting communication loss, which would point toward a shared wiring or bus fault rather than the ECM/PCM itself", "If wiring and power supply both check out, the ECM/PCM itself may need to be tested or replaced, though this should be a last resort given the cost and the need to rule out simpler causes first"]'::jsonb,
  faqs = '[{"question": "Is U0100 a serious problem?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 this is one of the more serious codes on this list because it reflects a breakdown in communication that many other vehicle systems depend on, not just the engine. It''s worth having diagnosed promptly rather than continuing to drive with multiple systems potentially affected."}, {"question": "Why do I have several warning lights on with this one code?", "answer": "Because so many modules rely on data broadcast by the ECM/PCM over the CAN bus, losing that communication can cause several unrelated systems \u2014 transmission, traction control, instrument cluster \u2014 to all show faults simultaneously, even though the root cause is a single communication network issue."}, {"question": "Could this be caused by something as simple as a loose connector?", "answer": "Yes, and it''s actually one of the more common causes \u2014 a corroded, loose, or water-damaged connector somewhere along the ECM/PCM''s power, ground, or CAN bus wiring can cause this exact symptom, and checking those connections is a sensible, low-cost first step before assuming the module itself has failed."}]'::jsonb,
  updated_at = now()
WHERE code = 'U0100';